title: 8. Why every change is reviewed, by whom, against what, and how much
pid: cc0a2efe-72ce-48e7-b7f4-fdd512adf199
body: |2-
  First use: the fresh subagent found real faults in four building
  missions of five, each after every suite had passed: a weakened test
  (Drew), a missed test name (Vic, part 2), an over-wide validation and
  untested cases (Vic, part 3), and in Cleo's part a wrong leaf for
  unknown viewnodes and a crash in the AI reader. A subagent costs no
  mission, so the cheapest check we have is also the one that found
  the most. Hence no change to the code, tests or docs goes unreviewed,
  planned or not (ours).
  Why a fresh mind and not the builder rereading: models struggle to
  correct their own reasoning without feedback from outside, and can
  get worse trying (Huang et al. 2023, read); and an evaluator scores
  outputs it recognizes as its own higher (Panickssery et al. 2024,
  read). A fresh subagent still shares the builder's model; it lacks
  only the reasoning. So a finding is trusted when it rests on
  something outside the model: a line of the plan it departs from, a
  line of code quoted, or a test that fails first (ours). Claude Code's
  own Code Review checks each finding against the code before
  reporting it (read); here the builder does that check.
  Why against the plan and the maps, and not for style: Claude Code's
  Code Review looks by default for "bugs that would break production,
  not formatting preferences" (read); Google's reviewers approve a
  change that improves the code's health even if imperfect, and mark
  the rest "Nit" (read). Understanding the change is the main challenge
  of review, and its main outcomes are as much knowledge and awareness
  as defects (Bacchelli and Bird 2013, read in the abstract); an
  owner's map lends the reviewer that understanding without a mission
  (ours).
  Why some 400 lines each: in Cisco's study people found most defects
  reviewing 200 to 400 lines at a time, and fewer beyond (read in
  SmartBear's account of it); Google holds 100 lines a reasonable
  change and 1000 too large (read). That a model's attention thins the
  same way is my inference, unsure. Several reviewing agents on one
  change is how Claude Code's Code Review works (read).
  Why the owner reads the commits later rather than reviewing: a
  review mission per change, by the owner, is the human practice
  (Google: a median of one reviewer, read in summaries) and here would
  double the missions. The owner's real gain is awareness (Bacchelli
  and Bird), which a later reading gives. First use: Vic asked Cleo to
  read the commits of his that changed her files.
